  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nA low pressure system near the west coast will maintain onshore \nflow with a deeper marine layer and cooler, drier conditions \nthrough Friday. Inland high temperatures will be 5 to 10 degrees \nbelow average. High temperatures will range from the mid to upper \n70s near the coast, the mid 80s to lower 90s for the Inland Empire\nand around 100 in the lower deserts. Night and morning low clouds\nwill be patchy, but may extend inland into portions of the \nwestern valleys at times. Low humidity and gusty westerly winds \nwill bring locally elevated fire weather conditions to the desert \nslopes during the afternoons.\n\nTropical Storm Marie is currently about 1,300 miles south\nsoutheast of San Diego. It is expected to move to the northwest at\nabout 9 mph and strengthen, becoming a hurricane on Thursday\nbefore beginning to weaken late Saturday. By Sunday, it is\nexpected to be about 775 miles southwest of San Diego.\n\nThe most likely scenario for southern California would be for \nsome mid and high level moisture from Hurricane Marie to be drawn\nnortheastward across southern California into the Great Basin by \nthe low pressure system off the West Coast. This would bring a \nchance of showers and a slight chance of thunderstorms from\nSaturday into next Monday.\n\nForecast details are still uncertain but a majority of ensemble\nmembers indicate periods of (generally) light precipitation for\nthe weekend, although brief periods of locally heavier\nprecipitation cannot be ruled out if tstms develop.\n\nWarmer and drier weather will likely return for next Tuesday.",
